\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Commuting constant links}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{Let the links be constant commuting unitaries. Restricting successive
unitaries to the eigenspaces of the preceding ones gives a common orthonormal
eigenbasis, by the finite unitary spectral argument in \ref{fdip-014A}.
On a common eigenline write the link in direction #{j} as #{\ue^{\ui\tau_j}}.
The forward shift of the [covariant centered differences](fdip-009O) uses its
inverse, so the character with momentum #{p} has shifted momentum
#{p'_j=p_j-\tau_j}. Its block is the Wilson block of \ref{fdip-014F} evaluated at
#{p'}. With #{t_j=1-\cos p'_j}, its squared length satisfies
##{a^2b(p')^2=\sum_j(2t_j-t_j^2)+\bigl(\sum_jt_j-1\bigr)^2
=1+2\sum_{i<j}t_it_j\ge 1.}}

\p{Thus every block is invertible. In the sign grading
#{\Gamma K(p')/b(p')} the scalar term is proportional to #{\Gamma},
whose trace is zero. The vector term is proportional to #{\Gamma A} with
#{A=\sum_j a^{-1}\sin(p'_j)\varepsilon_j^S}. Conjugation by #{\Gamma}
negates #{\Gamma A}, so its trace also vanishes. The positive and negative
eigenvalue counts agree on every block. By \ref{fdip-0155}, every constant
commuting connection and every gauge transform of one has index zero.}
